{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The appointed seasons of HaShem, etc.</b> [This is] the decree of the [divine] utterance. Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim) that it is ״אֲשֶׁר תִּקְרְאוּ אוֹתָם״ — meaning, they are ״the appointed seasons of HaShem״ only at the time that Israel proclaims them. Now one must know why it returned to say ״אֵלֶּה הֵם מוֹעֲדָי״. Further, one must know why it returned to give a commandment concerning the Sabbath. And further, I see that it returned to say a second time, after the commandment of the Sabbath, ״אֵלֶּה מוֹעֲדֵי ה׳״." "It appears that the measure of the verses is as follows: at the outset HaShem commanded that the essence of the appointed seasons depends on the times at which Israel proclaims them, and the verse was concerned lest they err to say that included in this is also the sanctity of the day of the Sabbath — that if they agree to postpone it from its day to another day, it would become sanctified by their word. Therefore it returned and specified <b>אֵלֶּה הֵם מוֹעֲדָי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים</b>, etc. — meaning: these, which are the Sabbaths, are My appointed seasons, appointed seasons that the Creator fixed, and they are not subject to change. And it returned to say <b>אֵלֶּה מוֹעֲדֵי ה׳ מִקְרָאֵי קֹדֶשׁ</b> — which I have permitted you that you yourselves proclaim them: in the first month, etc. — Pesach, Shavuot, Rosh HaShanah, Yom Kippur, Sukkot — these have no sanctity except by the word of Israel." "<b>Further,</b> it may be said [as] a reason why it repeated saying <b>אֲשֶׁר תִּקְרְאוּ אוֹתָם</b> two times, along the lines of what the Rambam wrote in chapter 4 of Hilkhot Kiddush HaChodesh, and this is his language: ״For three reasons the year is intercalated — because of the tequfah, because of the aviv, and because of the tree fruits.״ And there are other matters for which the court intercalates on their account: because of the roads, etc., and because of the bridges, etc., and because of the Passover ovens, etc., and because of the exiles of Israel who were uprooted from their place and have not yet reached Jerusalem, etc. — end of quotation. You see that for two general categories of reasons they intercalate and postpone the festival: one, on account of the conditions of the season, that it be in the aviv; and the second, on account of the people's fitness. Therefore the verse says ״אֲשֶׁר תִּקְרְאוּ אוֹתָם״ two times — and lest you say that only on account of the conditions of the season, which the verse conditioned as ״the month of the aviv,״ do they intercalate, but for the people's fitness the festival would take place at whatever times happen to be fit — a hermeneutic [proves otherwise, for] it says two times <b>אֲשֶׁר תִּקְרְאוּ אוֹתָם.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {2 "The phrase תלמוד לומר is a standard hermeneutic formula (literally 'the study teaches to say'); rendered here as 'a hermeneutic [proves otherwise, for].'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:31.873509634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}